C = [
("h1", "7. Deacons"),
("p", "Claim: Paul names a second office in the local assembly, the deacon, and he gives its qualifications in a list of its own. He says less of its work than of its character, and he does not say what a deacon is appointed to do."),
("sub", "7.1 The qualifications"),
("p", "The list opens with “Likewise must the deacons be grave, not doubletongued, not given to much wine, not greedy of filthy lucre; Holding the mystery of the faith in a pure conscience” (1 Timothy 3:8, 9). The word “Likewise” ties the list to the one before it, and the two lists share much. The deacon, like the bishop, is to be blameless and the husband of one wife, ruling his children and his own house well: “And let these also first be proved; then let them use the office of a deacon, being found blameless” (1 Timothy 3:10) and “Let the deacons be the husbands of one wife, ruling their children and their own houses well” (1 Timothy 3:12). The reward is stated: “they that have used the office of a deacon well purchase to themselves a good degree, and great boldness in the faith which is in Christ Jesus” (1 Timothy 3:13)."),
("p", "Two things stand out against the bishop’s list. The deacon is not said to be “apt to teach,” and he is not said to rule the church. He is to “rule” his own house, and that is all. The volume reports the omissions as the passage has them. John Piper draws the contrast the same way, saying that teaching and authority “are the very two actions that distinguish an elder or overseer or pastor” from the deacon. The volume notes the observation and keeps to the passages: the two lists differ in just those places, and Paul says nothing in them of why."),
("sub", "7.2 The word in the verse before the wives"),
("p", "“Even so must their wives be grave, not slanderers, sober, faithful in all things” (1 Timothy 3:11). The King James Version has “wives.” The word is the ordinary word for a woman and also for a wife, and the volume notes that the verse could be read of the deacons’ wives or of women who serve. The verse itself says “their,” which the KJV takes of the deacons. Paul does not say here that these women hold an office. The volume reads the verse as the KJV has it and records that the sentence leaves the point open."),
("sub", "7.3 Phebe, a servant of the church"),
("p", "“I commend unto you Phebe our sister, which is a servant of the church which is at Cenchrea: That ye receive her in the Lord, as becometh saints, and that ye assist her in whatsoever business she hath need of you: for she hath been a succourer of many, and of myself also” (Romans 16:1, 2). The word the KJV renders “servant” is the same word that is rendered “deacon” in 1 Timothy 3. Paul commends her and asks that she be assisted. He does not say she is an officer of the church at Cenchrea. The volume reports that the word is the same, and that the passage does not tell which sense Paul intends. It is no proof of an office and no proof against one."),
("sub", "7.4 What the passages do not say"),
("p", "Paul gives no list of the deacon’s duties. Acts 6 is not a letter of Paul’s, and the volume leaves it outside its scope. The letter to Philippi names the deacons beside the bishops: “with the bishops and deacons” (Philippians 1:1). The order of the names is the order of the greeting, and Paul does not say that the one answers to the other. The volume says that the office is real, that its qualifications are given, and that the work is not described."),
("ctbl", "The two lists set side by side.", [
    ("What is asked", "The bishop (1 Timothy 3:1–7)", "The deacon (1 Timothy 3:8–13)"),
    ("Blameless; the husband of one wife", "named", "named"),
    ("Sober; not given to wine; not greedy of filthy lucre", "named", "named"),
    ("Rules his own house well", "named, with the reason", "named"),
    ("Apt to teach", "named", "not named"),
    ("Takes care of the church of God", "named", "not named"),
    ("Proved first; not a novice", "not a novice", "proved, then use the office"),
    ("A good report of them which are without", "named", "not named"),
    ("Holds the mystery of the faith in a pure conscience", "not named", "named"),
  ], [0.40, 0.30, 0.30]),
("sub", "7.5 What the volume takes from the chapter"),
("bul", [
    "Paul names two offices in the local assembly, bishop and deacon, and gives a list of qualifications for each.",
    "The deacon’s list does not name teaching or the care of the church; the passages report the difference and give no reason.",
    "1 Timothy 3:11 and Romans 16:1 are read as written, and the volume reports the open points.",
    "The deacon’s work is not described in Paul’s letters.",
  ]),
]
